1	/**
2	 * A generic base class extended with no type argument, parameterised from the
3	 * installed declaration rather than from a remembered spelling.
4	 *
5	 * A library that adds a type parameter to a base class its users extend leaves
6	 * every subclass in the application uncompilable at the `extends` clause, and
7	 * every member the subclass inherited unresolvable behind it. TypeScript reports
8	 * the clause once — `TS2314: Generic type 'FieldType<F>' requires 1 type
9	 * argument(s)` — and then reports the whole inherited surface missing from the
10	 * subclass, so two type-position characters stand in front of a diagnostic
11	 * census many times their size.
12	 *
13	 * The argument to write is the question. A migration that answers it from memory
14	 * is writing a name the installed package may not publish, on a parameter whose
15	 * constraint it has not read. So this capability answers it from the declaration
16	 * the application is actually compiling against, and the reading it demands has
17	 * four parts, each of which is a refusal when it fails:
18	 *
19	 * - the base class really is generic in exactly the parameters the compiler
20	 *   named, and the parameter it is asked to fill declares no default — a
21	 *   parameter with a default is not the one TS2314 is about;
22	 * - that parameter carries a constraint, because an unconstrained parameter
23	 *   admits every type and so proves nothing about any of them;
24	 * - the installed tree publishes a companion type for the base class — the
25	 *   interface named for the class it configures — and that companion extends the
26	 *   parameter's own constraint, so substituting it satisfies the parameter by
27	 *   the declaration's own arithmetic rather than by resemblance; and
28	 * - the companion narrows the constraint by declaring members of its own, which
29	 *   is what makes it the argument the subclass wants rather than the constraint
30	 *   restated.
31	 *
32	 * The rewrite is then a binding-resolved type-position insertion. The identifier
33	 * at the compiler's position has to resolve to an imported binding, and the
34	 * companion has to be imported from the package that publishes it — extending an
35	 * existing declaration when the module already names that package, and only when
36	 * the companion's name is free in the module's root scope, because an import
37	 * that shadowed an existing declaration would change what the module's other
38	 * references mean.
39	 *
40	 * Nothing here knows what a formly field is. What it knows is that a class named
41	 * `X` whose type parameter is constrained to `C`, in a tree that publishes an
42	 * `XConfig extends C`, has one argument its own publisher wrote down.
43	 */
